"SDA: Steering-Driven Distribution Alignment for Open LLMs without Fine-Tuning (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16324v1)\nciteturn6689view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16324v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 828\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†Introduction L17:   3. cite7†Methodology of SDA L18:     1. cite8†Preliminary L19:       1. cite9†LLM Output Generation Mechanism L20:       2. cite10†Log-Probability and Logit: Positive Correlation L21:     2. cite11†SDA: Steering-Driven Distribution Alignment L22:       1. cite12†Score-Guided Amplification Factor L23:       2. cite13†Steering-Based Logit Realignment L24:       3. cite14†Divergence-Aware Dynamic Temperature Scaling L25:   4. cite15†Experiments L26:     1. cite16†Experimental Setup L27:       1. cite17†Models and Datasets L28:       2. cite18†Evaluation Metrics L29:       3. cite19†Comparison Pairs L30:     2. cite20†Experiments Results L31:       1. cite21†Universal Enhancement Across 3H Dimensions for Base Models L32:       2. cite22†Advantages Over Aligner Under Resource Constraints L33:       3. cite23†Flexible Control for Personalized Alignment L34:     3. cite24†Ablation Study L35:       1. cite25†Ablation Study Design L36:       2. cite26†Ablation Study Results L37:     4. cite27†Additional Experiments Results L38:   5. cite28†Related Work L39:     1. cite29†Training-Time Alignment L40:     2. cite30†Inference-Time Alignment L41:     3. cite31†Representation Engineering L42:   6. cite32†Conclusion L43:     1. cite33†Limitations and Future Work L44:       1. cite34†Broader Applications L45:   7. cite35†References L46:   8. cite36†A Appendix L47:     1. cite37†A Additional Figures and Illustrations L48:       1. cite38†A.1 Illustration of SDA on Output Distribution L49:       2. cite39†A.2 Illustration of Function $F(S)$ L50:       3. cite40†A.3 Illustration of Temperature Scaling L51:     2. cite41†B Further Details about Experiments Implementation L52:       1. cite42†B.1 Evaluation Datasets L53:       2. cite43†B.2 Evaluation Process L54:       3. cite44†B.3 Implementation Details L55:       4. cite45†B.4 Ablation Study Design L56:     3. cite46†C Additional Experiment Results L57:       1. cite47†C.1 Supplement Experiment Results of SDA L58:       2. cite48†C.2 Parameter Sensitivity Analysis L59: cite49†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L60: \nL61: arXiv:2511.16324v1 [cs.CL] 20 Nov 2025\nL62: # SDA: Steering-Driven Distribution Alignment for Open LLMs without Fine-Tuning\nL63: \nL64: Wei Xia ^{†}^{†}thanks: Email: xwisawesome@stu.pku.edu.cn    Zhi-Hong Deng ^{†}^{†}thanks: Corresponding author. Email: zhdeng@pku.edu.cn\nL65: ###### Abstract\nL66: With the rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs), their deployment in real-world applications has become increasingly widespread. LLMs are expected to deliver robust performance across diverse tasks, user preferences, and practical scenarios. However, as demands grow, ensuring that LLMs produce responses aligned with human intent remains a foundational challenge.\nL67: In particular, aligning model behavior effectively and efficiently during inference, without costly retraining or extensive supervision, is both a critical requirement and a non-trivial technical endeavor. To address the challenge, we propose SDA (Steering-Driven Distribution Alignment), a training-free and model-agnostic alignment framework designed for open-source LLMs.\nL68: SDA dynamically redistributes model output probabilities based on user-defined alignment instructions, enhancing alignment between model behavior and human intents without fine-tuning. The method is lightweight, resource-efficient, and compatible with a wide range of open-source LLMs. It can function independently during inference or be integrated with training-based alignment strategies. Moreover, SDA supports personalized preference alignment, enabling flexible control over the model’s response behavior.\nL69: Empirical results demonstrate that SDA consistently improves alignment performance across 8 open-source LLMs with varying scales and diverse origins, evaluated on three key alignment dimensions, helpfulness, harmlessness, and honesty (3H). Specifically, SDA achieves average gains of 64.4% in helpfulness, 30% in honesty and 11.5% in harmlessness across the tested models, indicating its effectiveness and generalization across diverse models and application scenarios.\nL70: Our code is available at https://github.com/adventurexw/SDA.\nL71: ^{1}State Key Laboratory of General Artificial Intelligence,\nL72: \nL73: School of Intelligence Science and Technology, Peking University\nL74: ## Introduction\nL75: The rapid development of large language models (LLMs), such as GPT (cite50†Radford et al. 2018 ; cite51†Radford et al. 2019 ; cite52†Brown et al. 2020 ; cite53†Achiam et al. 2023 ), LLaMA (cite54†Touvron et al. 2023 ; cite55†Grattafiori et al. 2024 ) and DeepSeek (cite56†Liu et al. 2024 ; cite57†DeepSeek-AI 2025 ) series, has dramatically advanced natural language processing across diverse domains including question answering (cite58†Zhu et al. 2021 ), text generation (cite59†Li et al. 2024 ), natural language inference (cite60†Yu et al.\nL76: 2024 ) and other domains. LLMs exhibit remarkably strong capability of generalization due to massive pretraining on vast amounts of data. However, as LLMs are increasingly deployed in real-world settings, ensuring that their outputs align with human intents—particularly in terms of helpfulness, harmlessness, and honesty (collectively referred to as the 3H criteria (cite61†Askell et al. 2021 ))—has emerged as a critical challenge.\nL77: Early approaches to LLM alignment primarily fall into two categories: supervised fine-tuning (SFT) using human-annotated data and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) (cite62†Ouyang et al. 2022 ; cite63†Chu et al. 2025 ). While both paradigms have shown promise in aligning model behavior with desired traits, they come with substantial limitations.\nL78: SFT is computationally expensive, requires large-scale curated datasets, and often needs to be repeated for each downstream task or model variant (cite64†Dong et al. 2023 ). RLHF pipelines, on the other hand, are complex, unstable, and heavily reliant on high-quality human preference data, which can be costly to collect and difficult to scale to personalized or domain-specific use cases (cite65†Xu et al. 2024 ).\nL79: To mitigate these challenges, researchers have explored more parameter-and resource-efficient alternatives, including prompt engineering (cite66†Sahoo et al. 2024 ) and adapter-based methods (cite67†Mundra et al. 2024 ). These techniques seek to guide model behavior with less training and overhead. However, they often fall short in terms of alignment intensity and generalization—especially when applied to diverse tasks and user intents.\nL80: Additionally, some methods attempt to expand the text search space (e.g., best-of-n sampling (cite68†Huang et al. 2025 )), leading to unnecessary forward computational waste (cite69†Dong et al. 2024 ). Moreover, all of them struggle to enforce fine-grained, interpretable control over token-level generation during inference (cite69†Dong et al. 2024 ).\nL81: cite70†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Overview of the SDA framework, designed to redistribute the output probabilities of the model, $\\boldsymbol{P}(x_{t}|\\mathcal{Q},\\mathcal{I})$, based on query $\\mathcal{Q}$ and alignment instruction $\\mathcal{I}$. Given a user query $\\mathcal{Q}$, SDA first samples an initial response from the base LLM and obtains an alignment score $S$ ($0<S\\leq 100$) for that response using an external evaluator (such as a stronger LLM).\nL82: Next, SDA converts the score into an amplifying factor $a$ via a smooth sigmoid-based transformation. Finally, SDA performs token-level steering to adjust the output distribution of the base LLM with amplifying factor $a$, while dynamically calibrating the sampling temperature $T$ based on JS divergence, enhancing alignment between model behavior and human intent.\nL83: Aiming to address these limitations, we introduce SDA (Steering-Driven Distribution Alignment)—a lightweight, training-free, and model-agnostic framework for aligning open-source LLMs during inference. The core idea of SDA is to redistribute the output probabilities of the model based on user-defined alignment instructions via fine-grained intervention on token-level generation.\nL84: Specifically, SDA reorients LLM behavior through a three-pillar adaptive alignment framework, operating directly at the probability distribution level via merely two tries of forward computation. It first identifies the directional gap between the base model’s output distribution and human-aligned intent via evaluation the performance of base model, which is then encoded as a steering signal to guide adjustment.\nL85: Next, it dynamically strengthens the human-aligned intent by calibrating distribution of the sampling token with the steering signal obtained. Finally, it refines the adjusted distribution using information-theoretic measures (Jensen-Shannon divergence (cite71†Fuglede and Topsoe 2004 )), adaptively sharpening the output probability distribution to bridge the gap between model generation and human intents.\nL86: The three-pillar design allows SDA to support both general-purpose and personalized alignment use cases, enabling fine-grained control over model behavior for different needs. It is compatible with various open-source LLMs and can be easily integrated with existing alignment pipelines. Crucially, SDA introduces no additional training or weight modification, making it both scalable and deployment-friendly.\nL87: In summary, SDA offers the following advantages:\nL88: \nL89:   * •\nL90: \nL91: Training-Free & Model-Agnostic SDA operates entirely during inference time, requiring no access to model weights or additional training, and can be applied to any open-source LLM that supports log-probability outputs.\nL92: \nL93:   * •\nL94: Interpretable and Flexible Control By leveraging a structured combination of evaluation-driven steering and divergence-aware scaling, SDA provides interpretable, token-level alignment without sacrificing generation quality or diversity.\nL95: \nL96:   * •\nL97: Significant Empirical Gains Extensive experimental results across eight open-source models and five datasets demonstrate that SDA achieves consistent improvements on 3H alignment dimensions—boosting helpfulness by 64.4%, harmlessness by 11.5%, and honesty by 30% on average—without incurring any additional training or computational cost.\nL98: ## Methodology of SDA\nL99: \nL100: ### Preliminary\nL101: #### LLM Output Generation Mechanism\nL102: LLMs generate text by iteratively predicting the next token $x_{t}$ from vocabulary $\\mathcal{V}$ (size $|\\mathcal{V}|$) based on the input context $x_{1:t-1}$. The process involves: (1) Context Encoding: LLMs process the input sequence $\\mathcal{C}$ and $x_{1},x_{2},\\dots,x_{t-1}$ through Transformer layers, producing a contextual representation that captures dependencies across the entire sequence.\nL103: (2) Logits Calculation: This representation is then projected onto the vocabulary space to compute output logits $\\boldsymbol{z_{t}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{|\\mathcal{V}|}$, where each element $z_{t}[i]$ represents the raw, unnormalized score for token $i\\in\\mathcal{V}$. (3) Softmax Transformation: Logits are converted into a probability distribution $\\boldsymbol{P_{t}}$ over the vocabulary via the softmax function:\nL104:  | $$P_{t}(i)=P(x_{t}=i\\mid x_{1:t-1},\\mathcal{C})=\\frac{\\exp(z_{t}[i]/T)}{\\sum_{j=1}^{|\\mathcal{V}|}\\exp(z_{t}[j]/T)}$$  |\nL105: where $T$ (temperature) controls the distribution’s sharpness. Lower $T$ (e.g., $T\\to 0$) concentrates probability on high-scoring tokens, making outputs more deterministic, while higher $T$ increases diversity by flattening the distribution. (4) Token Sampling: The next token $x_{t}$ is then sampled from $\\boldsymbol{P_{t}}$ using strategies like top-p sampling and nucleus sampling.\nL106: #### Log-Probability and Logit: Positive Correlation\nL107: \nL108: Log-probability $\\log P_{t}(i)$ and logit $z_{t}[i]$ exhibit a critical relationship: (1) Monotonicity: $\\log P_{t}(i)$ increases strictly with $z_{t}[i]$, allowing $\\log P_{t}(i)$ to serve as direct proxy for token preference. (2) Relative Comparisons: The logit difference between tokens $i$ and $j$ scales their log-probability ratio:\nL109: \nL110:  | $$z_{t}[i]-z_{t}[j]=T\\cdot\\left(\\log P_{t}(i)-\\log P_{t}(j)\\right)$$  |\nL111: Notably, $\\log P_{t}(i)$ is inherently non-positive (since $P_{t}(i)\\leq 1$), thus its value is free of sign ambiguity and its magnitude directly reflects the token’s relative likelihood. This property enables techniques like steering or intervention, where we modify $\\log P_{t}(i)$ to influence token selection instead of adjusting the logit $z_{t}[i]$. For short, we refer to the log-probabilities as logits in the following sections, and we use the two terms interchangeably.\nL112: ### SDA: Steering-Driven Distribution Alignment\nL113: Based on the above preliminaries, we propose SDA, a training-free and model-agnostic method that operates entirely during inference, to achieve efficient and flexible response alignment without fine-tuning. Our core goal is to redistribute the output probabilities of the model, $\\boldsymbol{P}(x_{t}|\\mathcal{Q},\\mathcal{I})$, based on query $\\mathcal{Q}$ and alignment instruction $\\mathcal{I}$. As shown in Figure cite72†1 , our SDA achieves this goal through the following three main components:\nL114: #### Score-Guided Amplification Factor\nL115: \nL116: Given a user query $\\mathcal{Q}$, we first sample an initial response from the base LLM and obtain an alignment score $S$ $(0<S\\leq 100)$ for that response using an external evaluator, such as a superior LLM. This score reflects how well the generated response aligns with user intent. To further use the information for the next step, we convert the score into an amplifying factor $a$ via a smooth sigmoid-based transformation:\nL117:  | $$a=F(S)=2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{1+e^{1-\\frac{100}{S}}}-0.5\\right)$$  |  | (3)\nL118: where $F(\\cdot)$ is a sigmoid function that maps the score $S$ to [0, 1], symbolized as $a$. This transformation ensures that when $S$ approaches 100, the amplification factor $a$ is close to 0, exerting minimal influence. As $S$ decreases, which means the original response is less aligned, $a$ grows and leads to stronger alignment adjustment in the following steps. For more details about $F(\\cdot)$, please refer to Appendix A.2cite39†A .\nL119: #### Steering-Based Logit Realignment\nL120: Based on the amplifying factor $a$, we perform token-level steering to adjust the output distribution. For a given query $\\mathcal{Q}$ and alignment instruction $\\mathcal{I}$, we compute the log-probability decoding token $x_{t}$ under two conditions: (1) $\\log\\boldsymbol{P_{1}}(x_{t}|\\mathcal{Q})$: the base log-distribution (without explicit instruction), (2) $\\log\\boldsymbol{P_{2}}(x_{t}|\\mathcal{Q},\\mathcal{I})$: the instructed log-distribution.\nL121: The steering vector is defined as the token-wise logits difference:\nL122:  | $$\\boldsymbol{S}(\\log\\boldsymbol{P_{1}},\\log\\boldsymbol{P_{2}})=\\log\\boldsymbol{P_{2}}-\\log\\boldsymbol{P_{1}}$$  |  | (4)\nL123: \nL124: We then integrate this vector into the base logits to produce a new adjusted logits vector $\\log\\boldsymbol{P}$:\nL125: \nL126:  | $$\\log\\boldsymbol{P}=\\log\\boldsymbol{P_{2}}+k\\cdot a\\cdot\\boldsymbol{S}(\\log\\boldsymbol{P_{2}},\\log\\boldsymbol{P_{1}})$$  |  | (5)\nL127: where positive integer $k$ ($k>0$) is a tunable hyperparameter controlling the strength of steering. This equation is intuitive: when $a=0$, no adjustment is applied, just the alignment instruction $\\mathcal{I}$ works. When $a>0$, the model output is pushed over the instruction aligned distribution $\\boldsymbol{P_{2}}$, as it encourages the model to generate responses that are more aligned with user intent.\nL128: #### Divergence-Aware Dynamic Temperature Scaling\nL129: \nL130: As Steering-Based Logit Realignment has already adjusted the logits to align with the alignment instruction, we could go further to amplify the alignment effect by adjusting the temperature to reshape the final distribution, making the tokens we want obtain higher probability during sampling. Thus, we introduce a temperature scaling mechanism driven by the Jensen–Shannon (JS) divergence between $\\boldsymbol{P_{1}}$ and $\\boldsymbol{P_{2}}$:\nL131:  | $$\\textit{JS}(\\boldsymbol{P_{1}},\\boldsymbol{P_{2}})=\\frac{1}{2}\\textit{KL}(\\boldsymbol{P_{1}}\\|\\boldsymbol{M})+\\frac{1}{2}\\textit{KL}(\\boldsymbol{P_{2}}\\|\\boldsymbol{M})$$  |  | (6)\nL132: \nL133: where $\\boldsymbol{M}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\boldsymbol{P_{1}}+\\boldsymbol{P_{2}})$. We choose JS divergence over KL divergence due to its symmetry and boundedness, which ensures stable and interpretable control. Based on the divergence value, the temperature is adjusted as:\nL134:  | $$T=T_{0}\\cdot\\left(0.5^{\\textit{JS}(\\boldsymbol{P_{1}},\\boldsymbol{P_{2}})/\\sigma}\\right)$$  |  | (7)\nL135: where $T_{0}$ is the base temperature and $\\sigma$ controls sensitivity. Higher divergence, equal to bigger difference between the base distribution $\\boldsymbol{P_{1}}$ and the instruction aligned distribution $\\boldsymbol{P_{2}}$, indicating the alignment instruction does have stronger effect on the decoding token, leads to lower temperature (sharper distribution), encouraging more deterministic outputs.\nL136: Instead, lower divergence indicates that the base distribution is already close to the distribution under alignment instruction, allowing for higher temperature and more diverse outputs. In case that $T$ is too small, we set a lower bound $T_{min}$ to prevent numerical instability. More details about the temperature scaling are presented in Appendix A.3 cite40†A .\nL137: Finally, the adjusted distribution is computed by:\nL138: \nL139:  | $$\\boldsymbol{P^{\\prime}}=\\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{\\log\\boldsymbol{P}}{T}\\right)$$  |  | (8)\nL140: \nL141: $\\boldsymbol{P^{\\prime}}$ is then used for sampling the next token. This process repeats iteratively for autoregressive generation.\nL142: In summary, SDA offers a simple yet effective framework for aligning LLMs at inference time, by effectively calibrating the output probability distribution of the model, $\\boldsymbol{P}(x_{t}|\\mathcal{Q},\\mathcal{I})$. It requires no additional fine-tuning, and introduces merely inference-level computational overhead. All operations are performed on the output distributions of existing models, making SDA highly resource-efficient and easy to integrate with any open LLM.\nL143: This lightweight design enables practical, scalable, and flexible alignment for a wide range of real-world applications.\nL144: Pairs  | Base Model  | Helpfulness  | Harmlessness  | Honesty\nL145: E-Dialogue  | DialogSum  | Beavertails  | HarmfulQA  | TruthfulQA\nL146: Empathy$\\uparrow$  | Reasoning$\\uparrow$  | Helpful$\\uparrow$  | Harmless$\\uparrow$  | Helpful$\\uparrow$  | Harmless$\\uparrow$  | Reliable$\\uparrow$\nL147: SDA vs. Base  | Llama2-7B-Chat  | 92.2%  | 23.4%  | 44.7%  | 13.0%  | 53.9%  | 5.8%  | 27.6%\nL148: 92.2%  | 23.4%  | 46.4%  | 13.2%  | 55.8%  | 5.7%  | 27.5%\nL149: Llama2-13B-Chat  | 87.1%  | 41.1%  | 45.6%  | 6.3%  | 54.8%  | 8.1%  | 52.7%\nL150: 87.1%  | 41.1%  | 52.4%  | 6.3%  | 62.7%  | 8.1%  | 52.1%\nL151: Llama2-70B-Chat  | 97.5%  | 11.1%  | 36.0%  | 8.1%  | 56.7%  | -1.0%  | 40.6%\nL152: 97.5%  | 11.1%  | 38.9%  | 8.1%  | 59.0%  | -0.8%  | 40.0%\nL153: Vicuna-7B-V1.5  | 73.1%  | 30.4%  | 28.7%  | 8.1%  | 24.5%  | -8.6%  | 32.4%\nL154: 92.1%  | 30.4%  | 57.7%  | 9.8%  | 58.4%  | -1.9%  | 34.9%\nL155: Vicuna-13B-V1.5  | 64.5%  | 48.9%  | 7.8%  | 22.6%  | 14.4%  | 25.0%  | 22.2%\nL156: 91.6%  | 48.8%  | 60.1%  | 24.0%  | 60.3%  | 34.9%  | 41.0%\nL157: DeepSeek-R1- Distill-Qwen-7B  | 60.9%  | 47.0%  | 29.3%  | 14.3%  | 46.1%  | 20.9%  | 9.8%\nL158: 66.2%  | 44.8%  | 39.0%  | 13.6%  | 49.8%  | 21.1%  | 9.5%\nL159: DeepSeek-R1- Distill-Qwen-14B  | 83.0%  | 60.6%  | 48.0%  | 25.4%  | 56.7%  | 21.1%  | 21.7%\nL160: 84.1%  | 60.6%  | 48.1%  | 14.4%  | 43.6%  | -0.7%  | 15.9%\nL161: DeepSeek-R1- Distill-Qwen-32B  | 95.1%  | 63.3%  | 61.6%  | 7.2%  | 67.5%  | 7.0%  | 19.9%\nL162: 95.8%  | 63.3%  | 70.5%  | 7.0%  | 70.9%  | 6.4%  | 19.3%\nL163: Average  | 81.7%  | 40.7%  | 37.7%  | 13.1%  | 46.8%  | 9.8%  | 28.4%\nL164: 88.3%  | 40.4%  | 51.6%  | 12.0%  | 57.6%  | 9.1%  | 30.0%\nL165: SDA vs. Aligner  | Llama2-7B-Chat  | 94.7%  | 41.6%  | 44.1%  | 9.0%  | 66.0%  | 10.9%  | 27.2%\nL166: 94.7%  | 40.9%  | 45.8%  | 8.9%  | 69.4%  | 10.1%  | 25.9%\nL167: Llama2-13B-Chat  | 89.2%  | 47.5%  | 41.3%  | 13.0%  | 52.3%  | 8.6%  | 48.4%\nL168: 89.2%  | 45.7%  | 47.7%  | 13.0%  | 59.9%  | 8.1%  | 46.9%\nL169: Llama2-70B-Chat  | 98.3%  | 17.3%  | 43.7%  | 10.7%  | 56.4%  | 8.1%  | 40.1%\nL170: 98.3%  | 16.6%  | 48.1%  | 10.6%  | 59.7%  | 7.1%  | 38.8%\nL171: DeepSeek-R1- Distill-Qwen-7B  | 61.5%  | 67.0%  | 35.9%  | -16.0%  | 60.4%  | -1.0%  | 8.7%\nL172: 66.0%  | 62.5%  | 43.4%  | -14.3%  | 65.6%  | -1.3%  | 9.2%\nL173: Vicuna-7B-V1.5  | 74.7%  | 51.4%  | 38.1%  | -17.0%  | 24.6%  | -25.0%  | 34.1%\nL174: 94.8%  | 45.9%  | 61.8%  | -13.5%  | 66.9%  | -17.5%  | 34.9%\nL175: Vicuna-13B-V1.5  | 62.4%  | 69.9%  | 9.1%  | 8.3%  | 17.5%  | 8.1%  | 21.7%\nL176: 89.9%  | 66.5%  | 61.9%  | 11.9%  | 67.0%  | 17.7%  | 42.5%\nL177: DeepSeek-R1- Distill-Qwen-14B  | 86.2%  | 75.8%  | 44.3%  | -4.6%  | 62.2%  | -16.0%  | 23.1%\nL178: 87.4%  | 72.7%  | 55.6%  | -4.5%  | 75.9%  | -16.1%  | 23.4%\nL179: DeepSeek-R1- Distill-Qwen-32B  | 96.8%  | 81.4%  | 57.8%  | -7.1%  | 65.8%  | -6.9%  | 26.4%\nL180: 97.2%  | 79.5%  | 72.6%  | -7.2%  | 82.0%  | -7.4%  | 26.0%\nL181: Average  | 83.0%  | 56.5%  | 38.1%  | -0.5%  | 50.7%  | -1.6%  | 28.7%\nL182: 89.7%  | 53.8%  | 54.6%  | 0.6%  | 68.3%  | 0.1%  | 30.9%\nL183: Table 1: Performance of SDA over base model or Aligner. The percentage value represents the improvement/advantage ratio (win rate) in performance between SDA and the base model or aligner on each test. Higher value indicates better performance by SDA.\nL184: As is shown in each cell of the table, we report two results: the upper one corresponds to the performance on full dataset, while the bottom one reflects the performance on the refined results, as the base model occasionally generates completely irrelevant responses or gibberish—such as sequences consisting entirely of punctuation or repetitive strings of irrelevant sentences—which are deemed uninterpretable or uninformative outputs.\nL185: To ensure a more equitable comparison, such cases are excluded from the refined results. More details can be found in Appendix B.\nL186: ## Experiments\nL187: \nL188: In this section, we conduct experiments on 8 different LLMs varying from source and size, to assess the generalization and scalability of SDA. The evaluation metrics include helpfulness, harmlessness, and honesty (3H), which are critical dimensions for assessing model alignment.\nL189: \nL190: ### Experimental Setup\nL191: #### Models and Datasets\nL192: Above all, we use 8 open LLMs of different sizes and architectures, including: Llama-2-(7B, 13B,70B)-Chat (cite54†Touvron et al. 2023 ), Vicuna-(7B, 13B)-V1.5 (cite73†Zheng et al. 2023 ), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-(7B, 13B, 32B) (cite57†DeepSeek-AI 2025 ). Besides, we use 5 datasets: E-Dialogue (cite74†Rashkin et al. 2018 ), DialogSum (cite75†Chen et al. 2021 ), BeaverTails (cite76†Ji et al. 2023a ), HarmfulQA (cite77†Bhardwaj and Poria 2023 ), and TruthfulQA (cite78†Lin et al. 2021 ).\nL193: Moreover, we use GPT-4.1 as the external evaluator to obtain the alignment score $S$, which is widely used and strong enough for evaluation tasks following cite79†Ji et al. (2024) . More details about datasets and model deployment details can be found in Appendix B.1 cite42†A & B.3 cite44†A .\nL194: #### Evaluation Metrics\nL195: To comprehensively assess alignment quality, we evaluate model outputs on three independent dimensions: helpfulness, harmlessness, and honesty. These criteria jointly capture both the utility and ethical reliability of responses. Specifically, we use win rate ($\\omega$) to measure the improvement or advantage ratio of SDA’s performance over others, which is a common practice in alignment evaluation (cite79†Ji et al. 2024 ).\nL196: And we use GPT-4.1 as the judge, which is asked to rate the outputs from 0 to 100 based on the 3H criteria. After obtaining the ratings, we calculate the win rate as follows:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn Interpretability-Guided Framework for Responsible Synthetic Data Generation in Emotional Text (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16132v1)\nciteturn6689view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16132v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 375\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAs If We’ve Met Before: LLMs Exhibit Certainty in Recognizing Seen Files (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.15192v2)\nciteturn6689view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.15192v2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 570\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.13182v2] Evaluating Large Language Models for Diacritic Restoration in Romanian Texts: A Comparative Study (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.13182v2)\nciteturn6689view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.13182v2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 202\n"
